# scout — environment template for EVERY domain: rent-watch, car-watch and job-watch. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
# NAMING (2026-08-29): every key that belongs to ONE domain is prefixed — `RENT_*` for the flats,
# `CAR_*` for the cars — so that a second watcher on the same account never reads the first one's
# database, label, topic or heartbeat. Unprefixed keys are ACCOUNT-level (IMAP_HOST/USER/PASSWORD,
# IMAP_SINCE_DAYS, IMAP_MAX_MESSAGES, SMTP_*, NTFY_SERVER, TZ) or TOOL-level (SCOUT_OFFLINE,
# SCOUT_MAX_PASSES, SCOUT_BACKUP_KEEP). The old unprefixed rent names (`SCOUT_DB`, `IMAP_MAILBOX`,
# `NTFY_TOPIC`, `HEARTBEAT_HOURS`, `FEED_SILENT_DAYS`) are REFUSED at startup, naming their
# successor — the same rule as the RENT_WATCH_* rename, for the same reason: a silently-accepted
# alias keeps both spellings valid for ever.
#
# THESE ARE LIVE SETTINGS. This preamble used to say the opposite — "nothing reads these yet, the
# adapters and the CLI do not exist" — and that was true when the file was written and false by the
# time anyone deployed. Every key below is read by running code today: `scout` reads them through
# `getenv()`, and `compose.yaml` substitutes the deployment ones itself before the container starts.
# A stale "this is only a sketch" notice on a live configuration file is worse than no notice, so it
# is gone.
#
# **Duplicate keys are a silent misconfiguration: the LAST occurrence wins.** Measured, not assumed
# — a scratch stack with `IMAP_PORT=143` above `IMAP_PORT=993` starts the container with 993. So
# editing a key in place does nothing if a second copy of it sits further down, which is exactly the
# shape this file shipped in until 2026-08-22. Fix the duplicate; never add one.
#
# `.env` is gitignored; THIS file is the committed template and carries no real values.
# CLAUDE.md hard rule 7: secrets live here and nowhere else — never in config/, never in a
# fixture, never in a commit message, never in a log line. Keep the two files in sync: a key
# added to `.env` and not to this template is invisible to the next person who deploys.

# ── Where the state lives ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The seen-set, the price history and the run log. Deleting this file makes the next run
# RE-NOTIFY EVERYTHING, and the price history is not reconstructible — a listing only ever
# shows its CURRENT rent. Back it up; do not put it somewhere a cleanup job will find.
# NOT under var/: that tree is documented as container-lifetime scratch for Claude reports and
# PreCompact handoffs, so `rm -rf var/` is an entirely reasonable thing for someone to do. The
# seen-set and the price history are the opposite of scratch — see
# CLAUDE.md § "Credentials & stateful data", which names this path.
RENT_SCOUT_DB=state/rent-watch.sqlite3

# How many verified backups `tools/backup-state.sh` keeps, pruning OLDEST-first. Bounded for the same
# reason compose.yaml bounds the container logs: an unbounded backup directory fills the VPS disk and
# takes the live seen-set down with it, turning the safety measure into the outage it prevents.
#SCOUT_BACKUP_KEEP=7

# ── Email-alert ingestion (the PRIMARY path for private portals) ────────────────
# CLAUDE.md hard rule 4 — this is within ToS, defeats anti-bot because there is no bot, and
# fires on publication rather than on a polling interval.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-22, AND TRUE AGAIN SINCE 2026-09-04 — in a narrower form. This used to read "a
# DEDICATED mailbox, not your personal one: rent-watch reads and MARKS messages". From 08-22 to
# 09-04 the second half was false: `ImapMailbox` opened the folder with EXAMINE rather than SELECT
# and fetched with BODY.PEEK[], so the server itself refused any modification. Since 2026-09-04 a
# `run` pass DOES mark: after the store has recorded a source's listings, the messages that source
# CLAIMED (passed its `params.from` and `subject_pattern`) get `\Seen`, in a second session, and
# nothing else — never a flag removed, never a move or a delete, and `doctor` / `tools/dump-eml.php`
# still touch nothing. The developer asked for it so the label shows which mail was processed.
#
# Two consequences of that flag, both worth reading before pointing this at a folder:
#   - the dedicated-label rule is now LOAD-BEARING rather than hygienic. Pointed at a personal
#     INBOX the parser would still only claim mail matching a configured sender, but a portal's
#     personal receipts from that same sender would be marked read under you.
#   - a message inside the window that STAYS unread is a signal: either no configured source claims
#     its sender/subject (a new portal template, a filter that widened), or the IMAP_MAX_MESSAGES
#     cap cut it. The flag makes truncation visible for the first time. Gmail flags are
#     per-message, so it shows read in INBOX too if the filter left it there.
#
# The SCOPE reason stands on its own: the parser reads every message in the folder it is pointed
# at, so pointing it at a personal INBOX means it parses personal mail. A dedicated mailbox is one
# way to bound that; the cheaper one on Gmail is a filter that labels the portal alerts and
# RENT_IMAP_MAILBOX set to that label, which is a folder to IMAP and leaves the inbox alone.
#
# TWO LABELS, NOT ONE — adopted 2026-08-26 after measuring the single-label shape. `rent-watch` held
# 1600 messages of which, in a seven-day window, 180 of 332 were THIS TOOL'S OWN notification emails
# (SMTP_TO is the same account it reads). Nothing was broken by that: each source's `params.from` is
# pushed into the IMAP query, so no source has ever read its own output or another portal's alerts.
# It is unreadable by a HUMAN, which is the reason to split it:
#
#   rent-watch/portails   the portal alerts — THIS is what RENT_IMAP_MAILBOX names
#   rent-watch            the tool's own pushes, subject-prefixed `[rent-watch]`
#
# Two things to know if you redo this on another account. A Gmail filter has no "remove label"
# action, so its retroactive tick ADDS the new label and leaves the old one — moving existing mail
# needs a search plus the *Move to* button, not *Label as*. And KEEP THE FOLDER NAME ASCII: whether
# `quote()` survives a modified-UTF7 folder is unverified, and an account that has ever had a
# non-Latin label (`&JxQ-` and friends appear in LIST output) is where that would first bite.
#
# Verify the new folder is POPULATED before pointing this at it. A label that is still empty makes
# every email source read zero, which is `warn_drop` immediately and `SOURCE_BROKEN` after three
# passes — noise in the one channel whose credibility hard rule 2 exists to protect.
IMAP_HOST=imap.example.net
IMAP_PORT=993
IMAP_USER=alertes-immo@example.net
IMAP_PASSWORD=
RENT_IMAP_MAILBOX=INBOX
# How far back each pass looks. Seven days, matching the source-health rolling mean so the two speak
# the same unit. This is NOT a performance knob — it is what "recent" MEANS. The fetch asks the
# server `SEARCH SINCE <date> FROM <the source's own sender>` rather than reading the end of the
# folder, because on 2026-08-25 a folder of 1436 messages answered SEARCH with 124 matches whose
# sequence numbers began at 6: the tail held none of them, and a live source read 0 listings against
# a mean of 9 while the portal published normally. A value of 0 or less is clamped to one day — a
# window that matches nothing is indistinguishable from a quiet market.
IMAP_SINCE_DAYS=7
# After how many days of PORTAL SILENCE a source that is still producing is reported feed_silent.
#
# It answers the failure measured on the live watcher 2026-08-28: `leboncoin` reported a healthy
# item_count of 3 on 263 consecutive passes, every one of them re-reading ONE email dated 26 August
# that SEARCH SINCE kept matching. No existing verdict could see it — the baseline was 3 and the last
# count was 3, so nothing dropped; no run failed; the schedule never stopped. It would have
# self-corrected only when the message fell out of the window, a week late and blaming the wrong day.
#
# KEEP IT UNDER IMAP_SINCE_DAYS. `scout doctor` says so as a WARNING LINE, never a refusal, and that
# difference cost a review round. This was a hard startup refusal justified by "the newest message
# SEARCH SINCE can match is at most IMAP_SINCE_DAYS old". THAT PREMISE IS FALSE: SEARCH SINCE filters
# on INTERNALDATE — when the server received the message — while this threshold is measured against
# the message's own Date: header. A message delivered today and stamped weeks ago (a bulk re-label, a
# delayed relay — the 2026-08-25 incident shape) is inside the window and arbitrarily old, so the age
# is NOT bounded by the window and the status is not unreachable, merely less likely to fire.
#
# The refusal also locked the tool out: IMAP_SINCE_DAYS=1 left no satisfiable threshold, so every
# store-opening verb exited 2 — including on deployments with no email source at all — and a refused
# `run` wrote a note meant to be reported on the next successful start, which could never come.
#
# The practical guidance stands: the observable band is (threshold, window), so on 3 and 7 you get
# about four days of warning before the count would collapse on its own.
#
# The default is MEASURED, not chosen. Cadences over 14 days on the live mailbox: Bien'ici ~30/day
# (longest gap ~4.5 h), PAP ~8/day, SeLoger 160 in seven days — none has ever been quiet for a day —
# against leboncoin, which has sent exactly one alert since it was created. Three days catches
# leboncoin and cannot false-fire on the others.
#
# STATED COST: a source firing thirty times a day is only noticed after three days of silence, which
# is ~90 missed alerts. A per-source override is the obvious answer and DOES NOT EXIST — adding one
# to a source block is a ConfigError, because unknown source keys are refused. Refused at 0 rather
# than clamped, exactly as RENT_HEARTBEAT_HOURS is: zero disables the only signal that tells a dead alert
# from a quiet market. That refusal is sound and stays.
#
# SECOND STATED COST: this reports that the SENDER sent something, not that the ALERT fired.
# params.from scopes a source to one address and portals use that address for more than alerts —
# leboncoin's only other message in fourteen days was a "Nouvel appareil détecté" account notice,
# which matches the filter and would refresh this date.
#RENT_FEED_SILENT_DAYS=3
# PER SOURCE, the same threshold is `feed_silent_days` on an `email_alert` block in
# config/rent/sources.json (2026-08-29): a portal firing thirty alerts a day is noticed ~90 alerts late
# under a global 3, while one firing weekly needs those 3 days. Absent, this global value applies;
# refused at 0 and on any source type that reports no feed date. `doctor` warns when either value
# reaches IMAP_SINCE_DAYS.
# ── The CAR domain (`scout --domain=car …`, 2026-08-29) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Its own database, its own mailbox FOLDER (same IMAP account — the sender routing fills it) and
# its own push topic, so a car never lands in the rent stream. Same rules as above: absent means
# the default shown; a key that already has a line here is EDITED in place, never appended.
#CAR_SCOUT_DB=state/car-watch.sqlite3
#CAR_IMAP_MAILBOX=car-watch/portails
# The car watcher's ntfy topic — a separate one, so the phone can tell the two apart and mute one.
# ONE NAMING SCHEME FOR EVERY DOMAIN'S TOPIC: `<domain initial>w-<32 hex>` — `rw-…` for rent-watch,
# `cw-…` for car-watch, the next domain the same way. Generate with `openssl rand -hex 16`.
# The topic IS the secret; never reuse one across domains.
#CAR_NTFY_TOPIC=
# The car watcher's own liveness beat and feed-silence threshold — same meaning as the RENT_ ones.
#CAR_HEARTBEAT_HOURS=24
#CAR_FEED_SILENT_DAYS=3
# ── The JOB domain (`scout --domain=job …`, 2026-09-13) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Its own database, its own mailbox FOLDER (same IMAP account — the `job-watch/portails` label fills
# it) and its own push topic, so an offer never lands in the rent or car stream. Same rules as above:
# absent means the default shown; a key that already has a line here is EDITED in place, never appended.
#JOB_SCOUT_DB=state/job-watch.sqlite3
#JOB_IMAP_MAILBOX=job-watch/portails
# The job watcher's ntfy topic — `jw-<32 hex>` under the one naming scheme above (`openssl rand -hex 16`).
#JOB_NTFY_TOPIC=
# The job watcher's own liveness beat and feed-silence threshold — same meaning as the RENT_ ones.
#JOB_HEARTBEAT_HOURS=24
#JOB_FEED_SILENT_DAYS=3
# How many messages one pass reads PER SOURCE. A cost ceiling, not a correctness one — it is also
# the number of bodies fetched on each of ~96 daily passes under `--watch`.
#
# It bounds the same query IMAP_SINCE_DAYS does, one by count and one by date, so the two disagree
# the moment a portal gets busy — and the disagreement is invisible: the newest messages are still
# read and the source still reports a healthy count. What silently shrinks is the CATCH-UP window,
# the thing IMAP_SINCE_DAYS is set for. Measured 2026-08-26: SeLoger matched 108 messages in the
# seven-day window against this default of 50, so seven days of stated resilience was really about
# three. Rather than raise the default and hide the trade-off, the fetch now SAYS when the cap bites,
# names this variable, and `scout doctor` shows the line.
#
# Clamped to at least 1, exactly as IMAP_SINCE_DAYS is: zero would read no messages at all, which is
# a source reporting a quiet market for ever. A non-numeric value falls back to the default.
#IMAP_MAX_MESSAGES=50
# OR, instead of IMAP entirely: point at a directory of .eml files. Needs no credentials at all, and
# is how the whole email path is exercised offline. Drop a real alert in here and it becomes a
# regression fixture. IMAP_HOST is used only when this is empty.
MAILBOX_DIR=

# ── Notification channel ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RULED 2026-08-07 (Q9): `console` is always available; `ntfy` and `email` are both supported and
# both optional. Enable a channel in `config/rent/criteria.json` under `notify.channels`, then fill its
# block here. Enabling one WITHOUT its credential is a refusal, not a silent no-op — but scoped
# (Q28): the process still starts if another channel is usable, and alerts you about the broken one.
#
# ⚠ OPTIONAL DOES NOT MEAN INTERCHANGEABLE. `console` reaches nobody — under Docker it is the
# container log — and neither does `email` over SMTP_TRANSPORT=file, which writes an .eml and sends
# nothing. Neither COUNTS as a delivery: a run with no other channel marks nothing notified,
# re-announces every listing on every pass, never writes the heartbeat marker, and `scout
# test-notify` exits 1. Not refused (a local `run --once` is exactly that shape), but warned about
# at startup and listed by `scout doctor`.
#
# The ntfy TOPIC IS A SECRET — anyone who knows it can read every notification. It travels as a URL
# PATH SEGMENT rather than a named parameter, so it cannot be masked by name: `Redact` masks the
# segment following the configured NTFY_SERVER host. If you self-host under a name that is not
# `ntfy.*`, set NTFY_SERVER here and nowhere else, so the mask knows what to look for.
# `rw-<32 hex>` — see the naming scheme under the car block below; `openssl rand -hex 16`.
RENT_NTFY_TOPIC=
NTFY_SERVER=https://ntfy.sh
# Telegram is NOT a supported channel under the Q9 ruling (console / ntfy / email). These are left
# commented so that filling them cannot produce silence with no error — an unsupported channel name
# in `notify.channels` is a config validation error.
#TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
#TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
# SMTP_TRANSPORT chooses HOW email leaves. Leave it empty for the sensible default: `smtp` when
# SMTP_HOST is set, `sendmail` otherwise.
#   smtp     — speak SMTP directly with the credentials below
#   sendmail — hand off to the host MTA (right under a compose stack that runs one)
#   file     — write .eml files to MAIL_OUTBOX and send nothing. No server, no credential; this is
#              how `scout test-notify` produces a real, readable message offline.
SMTP_TRANSPORT=
MAIL_OUTBOX=var/outbox
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
# tls (implicit, port 465) · starttls (upgrade after EHLO, port 587) · none
#
# `none` is REFUSED when SMTP_USER is set and the host is not loopback — a credential on a plaintext
# connection to a remote host is a credential on the wire. With NO SMTP_USER there is no credential
# to expose, so it is permitted; the MESSAGE still crosses the internet in clear, and `scout doctor`
# says so (`⚠ EN CLAIR vers un hôte distant`). This line claimed a blanket refusal for one review
# round after the guard had already been narrowed.
SMTP_SECURITY=starttls
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
# Leave EMPTY and each domain sends as its own label (rent-watch@localhost, car-watch@localhost);
# a value here is SHARED and overrides both.
SMTP_FROM=
SMTP_TO=

# ── Transit enrichment (door-to-door commute, spec §6) ──────────────────────────
# CORRECTED 2026-08-26: the key travels as an `apikey` HEADER, not a query parameter — measured
# against the live API, which answers 401 with {"message":"No API key found in request"} otherwise.
# Redact still masks `key=` and `apikey=` in adapter error text, which costs nothing and covers any
# provider that does put it in the URL.
#
# Get one at https://prim.iledefrance-mobilites.fr/ — personal menu, "My authentication tokens", the
# API tab, "Generate my token". It is shown IN FULL EXACTLY ONCE; afterwards only the first and last
# four characters. Quota 20 000 requests/day.
#
# ⚠ SET THE VALUE ON THIS LINE. Do not append a second `IDFM_API_KEY=` further down: DotEnv applies
# the FIRST occurrence and skips every later one, and an empty string counts as set — so an appended
# key is shadowed by this template line and can never be read. That cost a live debugging session.
IDFM_API_KEY=

# ── Income eligibility ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RULED 2026-08-07 (Q6): income-eligibility checking is OFF and NOTHING READS THIS. It is commented
# out rather than left blank so the template does not solicit personal financial data for a feature
# that does not run — the point of answering Q6 "off" was to keep the figure out of the process
# entirely, not merely out of git. Uncomment only if you overturn Q6; it is your revenu fiscal de
# référence, N-2, and it must never reach a commit, a fixture or a log.
#RFR_N2=

# ── Timezone ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RULED 2026-08-07 (Q34). The digest's daily emission is specified as "08:00 local", and a Docker
# container without TZ runs UTC — so "local" silently meant 10:00 Paris in summer. Every displayed
# timestamp and the digest boundary read this; `scout doctor` prints the resolved local time.
TZ=Europe/Paris

# ── The deployment (Q8: Docker, a mounted `state/`, `scout run --watch`) ─────────
# Read by `compose.yaml`, not by `scout` — Compose substitutes them before the container exists, so
# they belong in this file even though no `getenv()` names them. They were missing until 2026-08-22
# while compose's own comment said to set them here, which is the exact drift the preamble warns
# about: a key you are told to set and cannot discover.
#
# The image runs as its own uid and `./state` is a BIND MOUNT, so the directory belongs to whoever
# created it on the host. Mismatch them and PDO cannot create the database — observed, running the
# real container: the process died on an uncaught driver error. Set these to your own ids
# (`id -u` / `id -g`); 1000 is the ordinary first user on a Debian/Ubuntu VPS, which is what Q8
# prescribes. Equivalent one-liner if you would rather not persist them:
#   SCOUT_UID=$(id -u) SCOUT_GID=$(id -g) docker compose up -d
SCOUT_UID=1000
SCOUT_GID=1000

# Bounds `scout run --watch` to n passes and then exits cleanly — a diagnostic for a first
# deployment ("does one full pass work on this host?"), NOT a way to schedule. Leave it unset for
# normal operation: the watcher owning its own cadence is the whole of Q8's argument against cron,
# and Q37's pacing lives inside the loop rather than between invocations. The banner states the
# bound on every start, so a forgotten one is visible rather than mysterious.
#SCOUT_MAX_PASSES=
#
# One more variable exists and is deliberately NOT listed as a setting: `SCOUT_OFFLINE`, which
# `tests/bootstrap.php` sets so `CurlHttpClient` refuses every third-party host. It is a test seam.
# Setting it in a deployment would silently disable every source while health stayed plausible.

# ── Watcher liveness (Q27) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `scout run --watch` emits a low-priority heartbeat this often, whether or not anything matched.
# Without it, a dead watcher and a quiet rental market produce byte-identical output: nothing.
RENT_HEARTBEAT_HOURS=24
